{:edb/db-before-t 959 :edb/db-after-t 960 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534272 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:28:51.766Z" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188214 1071 "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188214 1071 "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188214 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} &= (12.5)^{2} + (11.7)^{2} + (13.2)^{2} + (12.8)^{2} + (12.1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 156.25 + 136.89 + 174.24 + 163.84 + 146.41 \\\\\n&= 777.63\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2$." 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188214 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} & =(12.5)^{2}+(11.7)^{2}+(13.2)^{2}+(12.8)^{2}+(12.1)^{2} \\\\  & =156.25+136.89+174.24+163.84+146.41 \\\\  & =777.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2.$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188215 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left(\\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188215 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188215 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188215 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$ \n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416925 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416926 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416930 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416932 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416924 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416927 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416928 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416929 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416931 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188459 1050 420.0 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185580 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416917 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188550 1071 "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.34,\\,1.24,\\,1.42,\\,1.28,\\,1.39,\\,1.44\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186188550 1071 "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$1.34, 1.24, 1.42, 1.28, 1.39, 1.44$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}$ for this particular sample.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186188550 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=1.34+1.24+1.42+1.28+1.39+1.44=8.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $8.11\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186189366 1064 "Recall that, given a random sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ of size $n$, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population mean $\\mu$, and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is an example of a **statistic**. A statistic is a random variable computed using values from a sample. Statistics are often used to estimate population parameters, although they also have other uses.\n\nLet's take a look at some other examples of statistics:\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population variance $\\sigma^2$:\n$\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline{X})^2$\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population maximum:\n$\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population range:\n$\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n) - \\textrm{min}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population median:\n$\\textrm{median}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$\n\nStatistics cannot include any *unknown* population parameters. For example:\n\n- If the population mean $\\mu$ is *unknown,* then the following quantity is *not* a statistic:\n$\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2$\n- However, if the population parameters $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are *both* known, then the following quantity *is* a statistic:\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(\\dfrac{X_i - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2$\n\nFinally, since statistics are described using observations from a random sample, they are random variables and therefore have a probability distribution. The probability distribution of a statistic is called the **sampling distribution** of the statistic." 13194139534272 17592186045417 false] [17592186189366 1064 "Recall that, given a random sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ of size $n,$ the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is an example of a **statistic**. A statistic is a random variable computed using values from a sample. Statistics are often used to estimate population parameters, although they also have other uses.\n\nLet's take a look at some other examples of statistics:\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population variance $\\sigma^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline{X})^2\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population maximum:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population range:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n) - \\textrm{min}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population median:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{median}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\nStatistics cannot include any *unknown* population parameters. For example:\n\n- If the population mean $\\mu$ is *unknown,* then the following quantity is *not* a statistic:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\n- However, if the population parameters $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are *both* known, then the following quantity *is* a statistic: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(\\dfrac{X_i - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, since statistics are described using observations from a random sample, they are random variables and therefore have a probability distribution. The probability distribution of a statistic is called the **sampling distribution** of the statistic." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416864 1080 "III only" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416864 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416864 1083 #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416865 1080 "2.8" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416865 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416865 1083 #uuid "0ca9c9f0-589c-52d3-a8c1-fba9d48743bf" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416866 1080 "I, II, and III" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416866 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416866 1083 #uuid "111f2a1e-49a4-