075 #edb/ref 17592186416886 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416891 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416904 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1077 "selection" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416874 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416922 1079 #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416879 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416881 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416893 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416896 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416912 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1077 "selection" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416879 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416923 1079 #uuid "f60edaef-c13b-5348-91f8-4e69a22c173f" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416922 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1069 "q-139820" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n- $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1072 #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416924 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416921 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1069 "q-139811" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1071 "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n2.25,\\,3.16,\\,2.65,\\,3.06,\\,2.94\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n})$ for this particular sample." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1072 #uuid "1599a91b-303e-58a8-b434-5ef4c1a036ef" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416925 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nTo find the median for the given sample, we arrange the data in numerical order:\n\n$$\n2.25,\\,2.65,\\,2.94,\\,3.06,\\,3.16\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{median}({x}_{1},…,{x}_{5})=2.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $2.94.$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416923 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1069 "q-149837" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1071 "The ages, in years, of six randomly selected college students drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n17,\\,19,\\,21,\\,18,\\,20,\\,19\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})+4}{n}\n$$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1072 #uuid "264130f3-38ba-510d-80d5-15a003677fae" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416926 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{min}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $\\frac{17+4}{6}=3.5$ years." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416916 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1069 "q-143552" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\mu \\cdot \\sigma -\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\mu -\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\sigma -max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{15})$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1072 #uuid "4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416927 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expressions I and II are *not* statistics. They are functions that contain the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416920 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1069 "q-139813" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1072 #uuid "5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416928 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions I and II are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416915 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1069 "q-139806" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}$\n- $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n- $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1072 #uuid "b59e493b-eefe-51a6-8a6a-b1643795634d" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416929 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416918 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1069 "q-139810" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1071 "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n12,\\,14,\\,9,\\,13,\\,12\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-12)^{2}$ for this particular sample." 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1072 #uuid "dea03ebf-107d-593b-bf22-bd12e437ac5e" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416930 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}({x}_{i}-12)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}((12-12)^{2}+(14-12)^{2}+(9-12)^{2}+(13-12)^{2}+(12-12)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+4+9+1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(14) \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $2.8.$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416919 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1069 "q-139814" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1072 #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416931 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416914 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1069 "q-149836" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1071 "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n11,\\,16,\\,17,\\,25,\\,19,\\,18\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{max}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})-\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})}{n+1}\n$$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1072 #uuid "f3ea39b0-84ca-56ad-ab8f-d5ad95ed12c9" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true] [17592186416932 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n- $\\text{max}({x}_{1},…,{x}_{6})=25,$\n\n- $\\text{min}({x}_{1},…,{x}_{6})=11.$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is \n\n$$\n\\frac{25-11}{7}=2.\n$$" 13194139534272 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" 17592186416864 "answer-0ca9c9f0-589c-52d3-a8c1-fba9d48743bf" 17592186416865 "answer-111f2a1e-49a4-5425-9c30-77e8f10a7813" 17592186416866 "answer-24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" 17592186416867 "answer-2e37b817-af08-5d0b-9574-36430ff431e7" 17592186416868 "answer-2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" 17592186416869 "answer-312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" 17592186416870 "answer-363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd" 17592186416871 "answer-3711c26b-4707-57d9-bc3a-ae24345bf877" 17592186416872 "answer-41c4124e-bec7-57ee-8cc8-21e077413d63" 17592186416873 "answer-4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" 17592186416874 "answer-4e0c5de3-7c79-5b7e-a720-d262f7cd52f4" 17592186416875 "answer-4e3fbe09-85be-535f-ad1f-23b1ff44f89f" 17592186416876 "answer-4e974b83-28b6-5e89-995d-50a977968dc8" 17592186416877 "answer-4fbcabc8-67e3-58c7-ada3-e4560b3ebf7f" 17592186416878 "answer-5851829e-e164-50e4-a521-b2e5029611e8" 17592186416879 "answer-5c159009-6903-5cb8-bc85-85d6ade859af" 17592186416880 "answer-61f7e4c9-a328-59a5-8c5e-a513b1c8edcb" 17592186416881 "answer-75b1e3b4-85ba-5f8a-bf46-da36377f8115" 17592186416882 "answer-7a32c857-a580-5328-90f1-f1b03c19902c" 1759218641